// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row closure_byvalue_param_never_dropped (tier 1). A CLOSURE'S
// BY-VALUE PARAMETER IS NEVER DROPPED AT THE CLOSURE BODY'S EXIT.
//
// `|foo: String| {}` receives ownership of the String and its body ends without
// using it. Nothing destroys it: 1 alloc / 0 frees, 22 bytes definitely lost,
// rc 0.
//
// THE FUNCTION DOOR IS CORRECT AND IS THE CONTROL: `fn eat(s: String) {}` called
// with the same argument reads 1 alloc / 1 free, 0 errors. So the epilogue that
// drops an unconsumed by-value parameter EXISTS — `SemaChecker` emits it at
// sema_decl.cpp's `emit_frame_drops(frame, epilogue_drops, &body_ever_moved_)` —
// and the closure-body lowering does not reach it. An arm that exists, missed at
// one of two doors.
//
// Found by tests/lattice/valgrind/sweep.sh; corpus member
// tests/imported/pass/closures/old-closure-explicit-types.logos (1 alloc /
// 0 frees, 8 bytes definitely lost, green through run_test.sh). Rust: exit 0,
// no leak — the closure parameter is dropped exactly like a function's.
//
// ⚠ THE ORACLE IS A DESTRUCTOR COUNT: 1000 = the closure parameter was
// destroyed, 0 = it was not. Today 0, exit 1; correct 1000, exit 0. THE
// FUNCTION CONTROL — `fn eat(d: D) {}` with the same counter — READS 1000
// TODAY, so the epilogue that drops an unconsumed by-value parameter exists.
//
// ⚠ SHARPENED 2026-09-07 BY THE CLOSURE LATTICE, MEASURED — THE DISCRIMINATOR
// IS THE CLOSURE BODY'S RETURN TYPE, NOT THE CLOSURE DOOR AS SUCH. Seven hand
// shapes on build febd1aa6e49ae30c 43:
//   LEAKS (count 0): `F: FnOnce(D)` void bound (this program) - `F: Fn(D)` void
//     bound - `F: FnMut(D)` void bound - a DIRECT `let f = |x: D| {}; f(d);` -
//     a void body with a statement that never mentions the parameter - TWO
//     unused by-value parameters of a void closure (count 0, not 1001) - a void
//     closure that ALSO has an owned capture (count 1: the capture is destroyed,
//     the parameter is not) - the same with a heap `String` parameter (valgrind:
//     definitely lost).
//   CORRECT (count 1000): the SAME unused parameter on a closure that returns
//     `-> i64` - a void closure whose body MOVES the parameter into a body local
//     - a plain void `fn eatv(d: D) {}` (the function control).
// So the door is not "closure bodies"; it is "closure bodies that FALL OFF THE
// END". ROOT NARROWED, by symbol: `SemaChecker`'s closure-literal lowering in
// src/compiler/sema_expr.cpp lowers the body with `lower_block(body_node)` and
// then `pop_scope()` — with NO params-scope epilogue. Its twin `lower_fn`
// (src/compiler/sema_decl.cpp) has exactly that epilogue, guarded by
// `if (!body_terminated) { … emit_frame_drops(frame, epilogue_drops,
// &body_ever_moved_); … }`, and its comment already states the rule this
// measurement re-derives: "A body with an EXPLICIT `return` already drops params
// via collect_all_drops (walks all scopes), but a void fn (or any fn falling off
// the end) misses them." The `-> i64` closures are correct because their bodies
// end in `return`. ONE CHANGE — run the same `!body_terminated` epilogue over
// the closure's param scope before that `pop_scope()` — closes all seven leaking
// shapes. Lattice cells h_bv_void_empty / h_bv_void_read / h_bv_void_stmt /
// h_bv_void_two / l_param_string_void against h_bv_i64_empty /
// h_bv_void_movelocal / h_bv_fn_control / l_param_string_i64 / f_param_*.
package closure_byvalue_param_never_dropped;

struct D { v: i64, c: *mut i64 }
impl Drop for D { fn drop(self: &mut D) { unsafe { *self.c = *self.c + self.v; } } }

fn as_buf<F>(d: D, f: F) where F: FnOnce(D) { f(d); }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut n: i64 = 0i64;
    let p: *mut i64 = &mut n;
    as_buf(D { v: 1000i64, c: p }, |x: D| {});
    let got: i64 = unsafe { n };
    if got != 1000i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
